WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.230
There's nothing quite like the anticipation
of a multi-mega-million-dollar movie making

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.780
its theatrical debut… and nothing like the
agony of watching it go down in flames.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.540
Not every big-budget Hollywood blockbuster
makes bank at the box office, and sometimes,

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.320
studios guess wrong about what will and will
not perform.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.680
Here are some of the films that have been
surprising disappointments.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.300
47 Ronin

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.720
With a large budget, a reliable kung fu fighter
as its star, and a plot that included both

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.689
samurais and dragons, 47 Ronin was supposed
to be the kind of action-packed blockbuster

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:32.700
that makes for a reliable end-of-year hit.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.720
Unfortunately, the behemoth of a movie turned
out to be too unwieldy for its first-time

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.920
director, Carl Rinsch.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.680
Plagued by problems and requiring extensive
reshoots, it was delayed by more than a year—and

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.640
despite the biggest, best Christmas push the
studio could muster, 47 Ronin only made $38

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.800
million in the US, far short of its $175 million
budget.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.320
John Carter

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.980
The insanely high expectations for this tragic
bomb of a movie may just be one of Hollywood's

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.270
great mysteries—because in hindsight, its
belly-flop at the box office should've been

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.380
a foregone conclusion.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.670
Taylor Kitsch was a popular cast member on
Friday Night Lights prior to being cast as

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:12.359
John Carter's main character, but he was hardly
a bankable star for a $250 million dollar

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.000
space opera.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.200
But that's exactly what Disney did—and the
problems were compounded by one of the most

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.140
grossly mishandled marketing campaigns in
the history of cinema.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.500
Ultimately, John Carter was an earth-shattering
disappointment that left the studio with a

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.720
whopping $177 million dollar loss in the US
before turning a small profit internationally.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.440
Still, it wasn't enough to keep the too-hopeful
franchise afloat.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.700
Battleship

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.680
The "you sunk my Battleship" jokes write themselves
when it comes to this $300 million albatross.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.459
Battleship had all the makings of a big summer
blockbuster, with a cast of well-muscled dudes

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:49.950
steering the ship, and high hopes of leveraging
the same '80s-kid nostalgia that made the

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.020
Transformers franchise such a giant moneymaker.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.229
"Missiles good to go, let's light 'em up."

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.200
But at the box office, Battleship suffered
from stiff competition from The Avengers,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.490
bad press, and a bad reputation for leading
man Taylor Kitsch, who you might remember

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.300
from his mention 55 seconds ago.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.600
Although the movie made back its production
budget once it hit international waters, it

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.720
still left Universal taking an $83 million
dollar bath.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:12.800
"Negative sir, it's a miss."

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.520
The Lone Ranger

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.160
Before Johnny Depp's personal reputation started
to sour, his professional presence was supposed

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.560
to be a reliable predictor of a film's success.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.780
So, with him sharing the lead, Disney pegged
The Lone Ranger movie as a big moneymaker

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:30.040
at the outset, pumping north of $215 million
into the production.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.580
But even once it became clear that The Lone
Ranger wasn't going to break any box office

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.340
records, its ultimate losses were wildly unexpected.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.300
The film ultimately didn't even make back
half of its budget in the US due to dismal

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:41.800
audience interest.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.220
"Okay, don't come see if I care."

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.040
The cast and crew blamed unfair reviews for
quashing ticket sales, but others pointed

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.180
to the whitewashing and stereotyping of its
Native American character Tonto as troublesome.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.660
"Perhaps you want to make it look like Comanche
violated treaty."

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.520
Ender's Game

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.340
Based on a classic science fiction novel by
Orson Scott Card, set in outer space, and

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.100
with a cast that included up-and-comer Asa
Butterfield and everyone's favorite star warrior

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.520
Harrison Ford, Ender's Game was a movie with
seriously high hopes.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:15.180
But despite the box-office promise of a big-budget
movie about teen soldiers battling hostile

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.980
aliens while the fate of the earth hangs in
the balance, Ender's Game was plagued by issues,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.590
including the effects of a boycott from groups
who were angered by the author's stance on

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.280
same-sex marriage.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.800
Between the bad press stemming from that and
mixed reviews from critics, it ended up barely

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.280
recouping its $115 million budget.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:32.860
Fantastic Four

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.640
After a slightly cheesy but successful pair
of Fantastic Four films rocked the box office

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.490
in the early 2000s, a fresh round of films
featuring Marvel's first family seemed like

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.100
just the thing for an up-and-coming director.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.920
Josh Trank, who'd done great things with the
found-footage thriller Chronicle, was tapped

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.320
to helm the pic, with a promising young cast
in tow.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.740
Producers were counting on the foursome to
become the next big thing in superhero ensembles,

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.640
alongside the X-Men and the Avengers.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.200
What they weren't counting on, unfortunately,
was for Trank to clash with the studio and

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:04.150
behave so erratically on set that Fox felt
compelled to reshoot huge portions of the

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:05.140
movie after the fact.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.560
The finished product was ghastly by all accounts…
which makes it unsurprising that the film

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:15.670
took in only $26.2 million on its opening
weekend and $168 million worldwide, against

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:19.160
an estimated $200 million production and marketing
budget.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:20.900
Jupiter Ascending

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.380
After The Matrix, everyone was on pins and
needles to find out what wild new world the

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.520
Wachowskis had cooked up with Jupiter Ascending.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.180
The film had all the ingredients of success,
including two of the coolest kids in Hollywood,

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.040
Mila Kunis and Channing Tatum, in the lead.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.580
This story of a cleaning lady who learns she's
the heir apparent to an intergalactic empire

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.110
had the full force and faith of big money
behind it, to the tune of a $176 million production

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.100
budget.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.340
"And you begged me to do it."

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.740
But the Wachowskis reportedly overspent on
the film, test audiences were unimpressed,

00:04:53.750 --> 00:05:00.010
and the critical reception was extremely chilly—all
adding up to a worldwide take of $184 million

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:03.420
that didn't compensate for the massive budget
and marketing costs of the picture.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.260
Mars Needs Moms

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.680
If you've never heard of Mars Needs Moms...
lucky you!

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.800
The name is a pun of "Mars Needs Women" a
60's Sci-Fi b-movie that was just as bad as

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.320
this one.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.840
On paper, it had all the makings of a hit,
including fresh visuals thanks to advanced

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.460
motion-capture technology, and a family-bonding
plotline with a cool alien twist.".

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.620
But nobody could've anticipated what an unmitigated
disaster the release would be.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:33.240
Not counting the cost of marketing, Mars Needs
Moms cost $150 million to make —and even

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.431
with international ticket sales, its total
revenue was just shy of $39 million, making

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.580
this film a planet-sized flop for Disney.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.120
Thanks for watching!

